      "content_preview": "![[Pasted image 20260203115248.png]]\nMedian = order from least to greatest -> value at the center point/position \nMedian = sum of value / value_num\n\n![[Pasted image 20260203115943.png]]\nTest - **A number calculated by a statistical test that describes how far the observed data is from the null hypothesis.**\n\n| **Data Type**   | **Definition**                                         | **Mathematical Operations**        | **Examples**                  |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| **Nominal**     | Categorical data with no inherent order or ranking.    | Counting (Frequency), Mode         | Gender, Color, Country        |\n| **Ordinal**     | Categorical data with a clear, logical order or rank.  | Comparison ($>$ or $<$), Median    | Education level, Star ratings |\n| **Categorical** | An umbrella term for Nominal and Ordinal data.         | Varies by sub-type                 | Occupation, Risk level        |\n| **Continuous**  | Numerical data that can take any value within a range. | Mean, Standard Deviation, Calculus | Height, Temperature, Time     |\n\n\n![[Pasted image 20260203120745.png]]\n\n\n\n|**Variable Type**|**Synonyms**|**Role in Research**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Explanatory**|Independent ($X$), Predictor|The variable you manipulate or use to predict a change.|\n|**Response**|Dependent ($Y$), Outcome|The variable that is measured; it is expected to change based on the explanatory variable.|\n|**Control**|Constant, Nuisance|A variable kept the same throughout the experiment to ensure a fair test.|\n|**Confounding**|Lurking variable, Extraneous|An outside influence that changes both the explanatory and response variables, potentially invalidating results.|\n\n![[Pasted image 20260203121532.png]]\nHeatmap\n\n![[Pasted image 20260203121729.png]]\n\n\n![[Pasted image 20260203122245.png]]\n\n\n![[Pasted image 20260203122439.png]]"
